"""Saint-of-the-day entries for Ora, September (original text). See tools/SAINTS_BRIEF.md."""
SAINTS_NEW = {
 "09-03": dict(name="Saint Gregory the Great", dates="c. 540–604 · Rome", match=["gregory"],
  history="A Roman aristocrat and city prefect who gave away his wealth to become a monk, Gregory was elected pope in 590 in a time of plague, flood and invasion. He fed the hungry of Rome, ordered the liturgy, wrote the Pastoral Rule and sent Augustine to convert the English. He called himself the servant of the servants of God.",
  life="Gregory was born around 540 into a senatorial family. After serving as prefect of Rome he turned his family house on the Caelian Hill into the monastery of Saint Andrew and lived there as a monk. Pope Pelagius II made him a deacon and sent him as envoy to Constantinople. Elected pope in 590, he accepted reluctantly and led penitential processions through the plague-struck city. With the empire weak, he organised food for the poor, negotiated with the Lombards and cared for churches in every region. Bede tells how he once saw fair-haired English boys for sale in Rome and said they were not Angles but angels. In 596 he sent Augustine and some forty monks to Kent. His writings on Job, on the Gospels and on the life of Saint Benedict shaped the faith of the Middle Ages. He died in 604 and is honoured as a Doctor of the Church and the apostle of the English.",
  prayerTo="Saint Gregory, apostle of the English, you sent missionaries to a far island and cared for the hungry at your door. Pray for the Church in this land, for our bishops and priests, and for me, that I may serve others humbly and bring to Christ those who do not yet know Him. Amen.",
  penance="Gregory organised food for the poor of Rome when the city was starving. Give to a food bank today, or share a meal with someone who would otherwise eat alone.",
  meditation="Gregory took the title servant of the servants of God, and every pope since has kept it. Whom are you called to serve today, rather than to be served by?",
  query="Saint Gregory the Great painting Zurbarán OR Goya"),
 "09-08": dict(name="The Birth of the Blessed Virgin Mary", dates="Feast · Kept in Rome from the seventh century", match=["nativity", "birth"], efMatch="Nativity",
  history="Nine months after the Immaculate Conception, the Church keeps the birthday of the Mother of God. A saint’s feast usually marks the day of death, the birthday into heaven, but the liturgy celebrates the earthly birth of only three: Christ, His Mother and Saint John the Baptist. The feast began in the East and was kept in Rome by the seventh century.",
  life="Scripture says nothing of Mary’s birth. The names of her parents, Joachim and Anne, come from the Protoevangelium of James, a second-century writing that the Church never received as Scripture but whose stories passed into art and devotion. The feast probably began in Jerusalem with the dedication of a church near the Pool of Bethesda, honoured as the site of her parents’ home, where the Crusader church of Saint Anne still stands. It spread through the East and was kept in Rome by the time of Pope Sergius I, at the end of the seventh century, who ordered a procession for it. Saint Andrew of Crete and Saint John Damascene preached on it. The liturgy sees in her birth the dawn before the sunrise: the child born today will bring forth the Saviour of the world.",
  prayerTo="Holy Mary, the Church sings that your birth announced joy to the whole world, because from you would come Christ, the Sun of justice. Pray for every child born today, for mothers awaiting birth, and for me, that the grace God began in you may also be brought to completion in me. Amen.",
  penance="Pray today for a mother expecting a child, or give to a charity that supports women through pregnancy and early motherhood.",
  meditation="The liturgy compares Mary’s birth to the dawn before sunrise. Where in your life might God be preparing a dawn you cannot yet see?",
  query="Birth of the Virgin painting Ghirlandaio OR Murillo"),
 "09-09": dict(name="Saint Peter Claver", dates="1580–1654 · Catalonia and Cartagena", match=["claver"],
  history="A Catalan Jesuit who spent nearly forty years at Cartagena, in present-day Colombia, the chief slave port of Spanish America. He met each ship as it came in, going down into the holds with food, water and medicine, nursing the sick and teaching and baptising the enslaved. He called himself the slave of the Africans for ever.",
  life="Peter Claver was born at Verdú in Catalonia in 1580 and joined the Jesuits in 1602. At the college in Majorca the elderly porter, Saint Alphonsus Rodríguez, urged him to go to the Americas. He reached Cartagena in 1610, was ordained there in 1616, and signed his final vows as Peter Claver, slave of the Africans for ever. Thousands of captives arrived in the port each year, chained and starving after weeks at sea. Claver went aboard with interpreters, carried food and medicines, washed wounds and stayed with the dying. He taught the faith with pictures, baptised, by tradition, some three hundred thousand people, and visited the sick in the hospitals and the prisoners of the city. Struck by illness in 1650, he spent his last years largely forgotten in his room. He died on 8 September 1654, and Leo XIII canonised him in 1888, together with Alphonsus.",
  prayerTo="Saint Peter Claver, you went down into the darkness of the slave ships to bring bread, medicine and the love of Christ. Pray for all who are trafficked, exploited or held in forced labour today, and for me, that I may not look away from suffering but go out to meet it with practical love. Amen.",
  penance="Give to a charity that works against human trafficking, or find out where the things you buy are made and by whom.",
  meditation="Claver taught that we must speak first with our hands and only then with our lips. What practical help could you give before you offer advice?",
  query="Saint Peter Claver painting"),
 "09-10": dict(name="Saint Nicholas of Tolentino", dates="c. 1245–1305 · The Marche, Italy", match=["tolentino"], efMatch="Nicholas",
  history="An Augustinian friar of the Italian Marche who spent thirty years preaching, hearing confessions and caring for the poor and sick of Tolentino. Known for his gentleness and his long hours of prayer, he prayed especially for the souls in purgatory and is honoured as their patron. In 1446 he became the first Augustinian to be canonised.",
  life="Nicholas was born around 1245 at Sant’Angelo in Pontano. His parents, childless for years, had made a pilgrimage to the shrine of Saint Nicholas at Bari and named their son after him. As a youth, it is said, he heard an Augustinian friar preach on the words love not the world, and asked to join the order. After his ordination he moved between several friaries before settling at Tolentino around 1275, where he stayed until his death in 1305. The town was divided by the feuds of the age, and he preached peace in the streets, visited the sick and the poor, and spent long hours in the confessional. Tradition tells that a dead friar appeared to him asking for prayers, and that he offered Mass for the departed for a week. Blessed bread, known as Saint Nicholas bread, is still given out in his honour.",
  prayerTo="Saint Nicholas, faithful friar and friend of the holy souls, you prayed for the dead as if they were your own family. Pray for all who have died with no one to remember them, and teach me to offer my prayers, my Masses and my small sufferings for them, trusting in the mercy of God. Amen.",
  penance="Have a Mass offered for the holy souls, or pray for the dead whom no one remembers. Share bread with someone today in Nicholas’s honour.",
  meditation="Nicholas spent long hours in prayer for those who had gone before him. Who among your own dead have you forgotten to pray for?",
  query="Saint Nicholas of Tolentino painting Piero della Francesca"),
 "09-13": dict(name="Saint John Chrysostom", dates="c. 347–407 · Antioch and Constantinople", match=["chrysostom"],
  history="The greatest preacher of the early Church, called Chrysostom, golden-mouthed, for his eloquence. Priest at Antioch and then archbishop of Constantinople, he preached the Scriptures verse by verse, defended the poor and rebuked the luxury of the court. He died in exile in 407. Eastern Christians still celebrate the Divine Liturgy that bears his name.",
  life="John was born at Antioch around 347 and studied rhetoric under the famous pagan teacher Libanius. After his baptism he lived as a monk and hermit in the hills until his health gave way. Ordained priest in 386, he preached in Antioch for twelve years, and his homilies on Genesis, Matthew, John and the letters of Paul are still read. In 398 he was made archbishop of Constantinople, almost against his will. He cut the expenses of the bishop’s household, founded hospitals, called the clergy to holiness and spoke plainly against the wealth of the powerful while the poor went hungry. He made enemies, among them the Empress Eudoxia and Theophilus of Alexandria, and was deposed and exiled twice. Marched in harsh conditions toward the Black Sea, he died at Comana in 407. His last words are recorded as glory be to God for all things.",
  prayerTo="Saint John Chrysostom, you preached the word of God without fear and saw Christ in the poor at the church door. Pray for all preachers, that they may speak the truth with love, and for me, that I may honour Christ in the Eucharist and in those who are hungry, and give thanks to God for all things. Amen.",
  penance="Read and ponder one chapter of Saint Matthew’s Gospel today, on which John preached at length, and give the price of one small luxury to the poor.",
  meditation="Chrysostom warned his people not to honour Christ in church with silks while neglecting Him naked and cold outside. Where is Christ waiting for you beyond the church door today?",
  query="Saint John Chrysostom painting"),
 "09-14": dict(name="The Exaltation of the Holy Cross", dates="Jerusalem, 335", match=["cross"], efMatch="Cross",
  history="The feast keeps the anniversary of the dedication of the church of the Holy Sepulchre in Jerusalem in 335, built by Constantine over the place of the crucifixion and the tomb. Tradition holds that his mother, Saint Helena, had found the wood of the true cross there. The cross, once a gallows, is lifted up as the throne of Christ’s victory.",
  life="Eusebius records the building of Constantine’s basilica at Golgotha and its dedication in September 335. Later in that century the pilgrim Egeria described the great crowds who came for the anniversary, and the faithful filing past one by one to kiss the relic of the cross on Good Friday. The story that Helena discovered the cross, around 326, is told by writers of the late fourth century, among them Saint Ambrose. In 614 the Persians sacked Jerusalem and carried the relic away; the Emperor Heraclius recovered it and restored it to the city around 630, and this too came to be remembered on the feast. Exaltation means lifting up: Christ said that when He was lifted up from the earth He would draw all people to Himself. Eastern Christians keep the day with fasting and a solemn veneration of the cross.",
  prayer="We adore thee, O Christ, and we bless thee, because by thy holy cross thou hast redeemed the world.",
  prayerSource="Traditional antiphon, Adoramus te, Christe",
  prayerTo="Lord Jesus Christ, you were lifted up on the cross to draw all people to yourself. Through the prayers of Our Lady, who stood beside it, help me to embrace the crosses of my own life, to see in them a share in your love, and to glory in nothing but your cross, by which the world is redeemed. Amen.",
  penance="Abstain from meat today, as on a Friday, and pray the Stations of the Cross or spend a few quiet minutes before a crucifix.",
  meditation="When I am lifted up from the earth, I will draw all to myself. Which cross in your life might be the place where Christ is drawing you closer?",
  query="Piero della Francesca Legend of the True Cross Exaltation"),
 "09-15": dict(name="Our Lady of Sorrows", dates="Feast extended to the whole Church in 1814", match=["sorrows"], efMatch="Sorrows",
  history="The day after the Exaltation of the Cross, the Church stands with Mary beneath it. Simeon had told her that a sword would pierce her soul, and tradition counts seven sorrows, from his prophecy to the burial of her Son. Mary suffered with Christ, and her faith did not fail. The Servite friars did much to spread the devotion.",
  life="The seven sorrows traditionally honoured are the prophecy of Simeon, the flight into Egypt, the loss of the child Jesus in the Temple, the meeting with Jesus on the way to Calvary, the crucifixion, the taking down from the cross and the burial. Meditation on Mary’s compassion grew strongly in the Middle Ages, when the Stabat Mater was written, often attributed to the Franciscan Jacopone da Todi. The Order of Servants of Mary, founded in Florence in the thirteenth century by seven merchants, made the sorrows of Our Lady their particular devotion. Pius VII, on his return to Rome after captivity under Napoleon, extended the feast to the whole Church in 1814 in thanksgiving, and Pius X fixed it on 15 September. The Servite chaplet of the seven sorrows, seven groups of seven Hail Marys, is still widely prayed.",
  prayerTo="Holy Mother of Sorrows, you stood by the cross when others fled, and your faith held firm in the darkest hour. Stay beside all who keep watch at a deathbed, all parents who have lost a child, and all whose hearts are pierced. Pray for me, that I may stand by Christ in His suffering members. Amen.",
  penance="Pray the seven sorrows today, one Hail Mary for each, and visit, call or write to someone who is grieving.",
  meditation="Mary could not take away her Son’s suffering, but she stayed. Whose suffering are you being asked simply to stay beside?",
  query="Mater Dolorosa painting Titian"),
 "09-16": dict(name="Saints Cornelius and Cyprian", dates="Died 253 and 258 · Rome and Carthage", match=["cornelius", "cyprian"], efMatch="Cornelius",
  history="A pope and a bishop of Carthage, allies in a time of persecution, both named in the Roman Canon. When Christians who had denied the faith under threat of death sought to return, Cornelius and Cyprian upheld both mercy and penance against a rigorist schism. Cornelius died in exile in 253; Cyprian was beheaded in 258.",
  life="Under the Emperor Decius in 250 many Christians gave way and offered sacrifice to the gods. When the persecution eased, the Church had to decide how to receive them back. Cornelius, elected Bishop of Rome in 251, held that the lapsed could be reconciled after penance. The priest Novatian, taking a harsher view, had himself made a rival bishop. Cyprian, a rhetorician who had become a Christian as a grown man and soon afterwards bishop of Carthage, stood by Cornelius and wrote his treatise On the Unity of the Catholic Church. Cornelius was exiled to Centumcellae, on the coast north of Rome, where he died in 253. Under the Emperor Valerian, Cyprian was arrested and condemned. Hearing the sentence, he answered thanks be to God, and had twenty-five gold pieces given to his executioner.",
  prayerTo="Saints Cornelius and Cyprian, shepherds in a time of trial, you held together mercy for the fallen and fidelity to Christ. Pray for the unity of the Church, for those who have drifted from the faith and long to return, and for me, that I may never be ashamed of Christ, whatever it may cost. Amen.",
  penance="Go to confession, or make a careful examination of conscience tonight, and pray for someone who has fallen away from the faith.",
  meditation="Cyprian wrote that no one can have God for his Father who does not have the Church for his mother. How do you love the Church, with all her weakness?",
  query="Veronese Saints Cornelius Anthony Abbot and Cyprian"),
 "09-17": dict(name="Saint Robert Bellarmine", dates="1542–1621 · Montepulciano and Rome", match=["bellarmine"],
  history="A Jesuit theologian and cardinal from Montepulciano, the foremost defender of Catholic teaching in the controversies after the Reformation. His catechisms taught children for three centuries. He lived poorly, gave freely to the poor, and guided the young Aloysius Gonzaga. He was canonised in 1930 and named a Doctor of the Church a year later.",
  life="Robert Bellarmine was born in 1542, a nephew of Pope Marcellus II, and entered the Jesuits in 1560. Small, often unwell and quick-witted, he taught first at Louvain and then at the Roman College, where his lectures became the Controversies, a vast and careful statement of Catholic teaching on the questions disputed with the Reformers. He was known for setting out his opponents’ arguments fairly. Clement VIII made him a cardinal in 1599, and he served for three years as archbishop of Capua. In 1616 he was involved in the Church’s first inquiry into Galileo’s teaching. In Rome he kept a simple household, gave away most of his income, and wrote short spiritual books, among them The Art of Dying Well. He died in 1621, asking to be laid near Aloysius Gonzaga, whose confessor he had been; they now rest close together in Sant’Ignazio.",
  prayerTo="Saint Robert, you served the truth with learning, patience and courtesy, and lived simply among the riches of Rome. Pray for teachers of the faith and for all who seek the truth in good conscience, and for me, that I may know my faith well, speak of it with charity, and prepare each day to die well. Amen.",
  penance="Read a page of the Catechism today, and give away something of which you have more than you need.",
  meditation="Bellarmine taught that the art of dying well is learned by living well. If today were your last, what would you want to put right?",
  query="Saint Robert Bellarmine portrait painting"),
 "09-18": dict(name="Saint Joseph of Cupertino", dates="1603–1663 · Apulia and the Marche", match=["cupertino"], efMatch="Cupertino",
  history="A Franciscan friar of southern Italy, so slow at his books and clumsy at his work that he was sent away by one order and barely accepted by another. Ordained priest, he was caught up in ecstasy so often, and was seen by so many witnesses to rise from the ground in prayer, that he was kept away from crowds. He is a patron of students.",
  life="Joseph Desa was born in 1603 at Copertino in Apulia, in a shed, it is said, because his father’s debts had cost the family their home. A sickly, absent-minded boy, he was nicknamed Boccaperta, open-mouth. The Capuchins took him as a lay brother but sent him away after eight months for his clumsiness. The Conventual Franciscans at La Grottella accepted him to look after their mule, and his humility and prayer won him a place in the novitiate. He struggled with study, and tradition tells that at each examination he was questioned only on the one text he knew well; he was ordained in 1628. His ecstasies, in which he was seen to rise into the air, drew such crowds that the Inquisition examined him, and he was moved from friary to friary, ending at Osimo, where he died in 1663. He was canonised in 1767.",
  prayerTo="Saint Joseph of Cupertino, you were thought a fool and found your joy in God alone. Pray for all students, especially those who struggle and fear failure, and for me, that I may do my best with the gifts I have, accept my limits with humility, and let my heart be lifted to God in prayer. Amen.",
  penance="Do a humble task today that you would normally leave to others, and pray for a student facing exams.",
  meditation="Joseph had little learning but a great love for God. Do you trust that God values your love more than your achievements?",
  query="Joseph of Cupertino levitation painting Mazzanti"),
 "09-19": dict(name="Saint Januarius", dates="Died c. 305 · Benevento and Naples", match=["januarius"],
  history="Bishop of Benevento, martyred near Naples in the persecution of Diocletian around 305, and venerated as the patron of that city. A phial said to hold his blood is kept in Naples cathedral; on his feast and on other days each year the dried mass has repeatedly been seen to liquefy. The Church permits the devotion without pronouncing on its cause.",
  life="Little is certain about Januarius beyond his martyrdom, and the accounts of his death were written long afterwards. Tradition holds that he went to visit Christians imprisoned at Pozzuoli, was arrested himself, was thrown to wild beasts that would not touch him, and was beheaded near the Solfatara with several companions. His relics were moved more than once over the centuries and came back to Naples for good in 1497. The liquefaction of the blood was first recorded in 1389 and has been observed many times since, usually on the Saturday before the first Sunday of May, on 19 September and on 16 December, before crowds who pray for it. Neapolitans credit him with protecting their city, especially during the eruption of Vesuvius in 1631. Scientists have proposed explanations, and the Church has made no formal judgement: the devotion rests on faith and prayer, not on dogma.",
  prayerTo="Saint Januarius, bishop and martyr, you gave your life for Christ, and your people have trusted in your prayers for centuries. Pray for the city of Naples and for all who live in the shadow of danger, and for me, that my faith may not be dry and hard but warm and living. Amen.",
  penance="Pray today for Christians who face death for their faith, and give alms in their name to a charity that supports the persecuted Church.",
  meditation="Each year the people of Naples wait and pray for the blood to liquefy. Is there a hardened place in your heart that you could ask God to soften?",
  query="Saint Januarius Emerging Unscathed from the Furnace Ribera"),
 "09-20": dict(name="Saints Andrew Kim Taegon, Paul Chong Hasang and Companions", dates="Martyred 1839–1867 · Korea", match=["taegon", "hasang"],
  history="The Church in Korea was founded by lay people, scholars who found the faith in Christian books from China in the late eighteenth century. For decades it grew under persecution, often without priests, and thousands died. The 103 martyrs canonised in 1984 include Andrew Kim Taegon, the first Korean-born priest, and the lay leader Paul Chong Hasang.",
  life="In 1784 Yi Seung-hun, baptised in Beijing, came home to Korea and began baptising others. The young community spread the faith by itself for years and sent messengers again and again to beg for priests. Under a government that saw the new religion as a threat to the social order, it suffered waves of persecution. Paul Chong Hasang, whose father and brother had been martyred, travelled many times to Beijing to ask for clergy, and wrote a defence of the faith addressed to the authorities before his execution in 1839. Andrew Kim Taegon, son of a martyr, studied in Macau and was ordained in Shanghai in 1845. Back in Korea he served for barely a year before he was arrested and beheaded near Seoul in 1846, aged twenty-five. Among the 103 saints are French missionary bishops and priests, but most were lay men, women and children. John Paul II canonised them in Seoul in 1984.",
  prayerTo="Holy martyrs of Korea, you received the faith from books and handed it on with your blood. Pray for the Church in Korea, for all who must practise their faith in secret, and for me, that I may treasure the faith I received so easily and share it with courage and joy. Amen.",
  penance="Pray today for Christians who live under persecution, and offer a small sacrifice, such as a treat given up, for those who worship in secret.",
  meditation="The first Korean Catholics found Christ in the pages of a book and kept the faith alone for years. What have you done with the faith that was handed to you?",
  query="Andrew Kim Taegon portrait"),
 "09-21": dict(name="Saint Matthew the Apostle", dates="First century · Apostle and Evangelist", match=["matthew"], efMatch="Matthew",
  history="A tax collector at Capernaum, despised as a collaborator with Rome, whom Jesus called from his booth with two words: follow me. He left everything, gave a feast for his old companions, and heard Christ say that He had come to call sinners. Tradition holds him to be the author of the first Gospel, written for Jewish Christians.",
  life="Mark and Luke call him Levi, son of Alphaeus; in the Gospel that bears his name he is Matthew the tax collector. Collecting customs by the Sea of Galilee, he would have been rich and unwelcome. When Jesus called him, he rose and followed, and soon invited Jesus to dine with tax collectors and sinners, which scandalised the Pharisees. Early writers, beginning with Papias, say that Matthew gathered the sayings of the Lord in the Hebrew language. His Gospel shows Jesus as the one in whom the Law and the Prophets are fulfilled, and gives us the visit of the Magi, the Sermon on the Mount, the Lord’s Prayer in the form we pray it and the command to make disciples of all nations. Traditions differ on where he preached and died, naming Ethiopia or Persia. His symbol is a winged man.",
  prayerTo="Saint Matthew, you left your counting table at a word from Christ and opened your house to sinners. Pray for all whose work is with money, that they may be honest and generous, and for me, that I may hear the Lord’s call today, rise without delay, and follow Him. Amen.",
  penance="Look honestly at your spending and give a generous share of something to the poor today, or settle a debt you have put off.",
  meditation="Jesus saw a man called Matthew sitting at the tax office and said to him, follow me. What keeps you sitting at your table when Christ calls?",
  query="Calling of Saint Matthew Caravaggio"),
 "09-23": dict(name="Saint Pio of Pietrelcina", dates="1887–1968 · Pietrelcina and San Giovanni Rotondo", match=["pio"],
  history="A Capuchin friar who for fifty years bore the wounds of Christ in his hands, feet and side. From the friary of San Giovanni Rotondo in southern Italy he heard confessions for many hours a day, drew pilgrims from all over the world, and founded a great hospital for the relief of suffering. He was canonised in 2002.",
  life="Francesco Forgione was born at Pietrelcina, near Benevento, in 1887, the son of farmers. He entered the Capuchin novitiate at fifteen, taking the name Pio, and was ordained in 1910. Poor health kept him at home for long periods, and in 1916 he was sent to San Giovanni Rotondo, where he spent the rest of his life. On 20 September 1918, praying before a crucifix, he received the visible stigmata. The wounds were examined by doctors and by the Holy See, and in the early 1930s, while the case was investigated, he was forbidden for a time to say Mass in public or to hear confessions. He obeyed. Crowds came to his Mass and to his confessional, where he could be gentle or severe. He encouraged prayer groups and built the hospital Casa Sollievo della Sofferenza, opened in 1956. He died on 23 September 1968.",
  prayerTo="Saint Pio, you shared the wounds of Christ and spent your days reconciling sinners to God. Pray for all who are sick and all who care for them, for priests in the confessional, and for me, that I may make a good confession, carry my cross without complaint, and learn to pray, hope and not worry. Amen.",
  penance="Go to confession this week if you can, and pray the Rosary today for someone who is ill.",
  meditation="Pray, hope and don’t worry, he told those who came to him. Which worry could you hand over to God in prayer today?",
  query="Padre Pio portrait"),
 "09-27": dict(name="Saint Vincent de Paul", dates="1581–1660 · Gascony and Paris", match=["vincent"],
  history="A Gascon farmer’s son who became a priest hoping for a comfortable living, and was converted to the service of the poor. He founded the Congregation of the Mission to preach to country people and, with Saint Louise de Marillac, the Daughters of Charity. He is the patron of all works of charity.",
  life="Vincent was born in 1581 at Pouy, in south-west France, and ordained young, in 1600, hoping for a good benefice. His own letters tell of capture by pirates and slavery in Tunis, though historians debate the story. As chaplain to the powerful Gondi family he came to see the spiritual neglect of the peasants on their estates. In 1617 a dying man’s confession at Folleville, and a sick family in his parish at Châtillon, turned his life around: he preached missions and gathered wealthy women into Confraternities of Charity. In 1625 he founded the Congregation of the Mission, and in 1633, with Louise de Marillac, the Daughters of Charity, sisters who served the sick in their homes and in the streets rather than behind a cloister. He cared for galley slaves, foundlings and refugees of war, and reformed the training of priests. He died in Paris in 1660.",
  prayerTo="Saint Vincent de Paul, you learned to see Christ in the poor and spent all your strength in serving them. Pray for all who work for the poor, the sick and the abandoned, and for me, that my charity may be practical and humble, and that I may love the poor as my masters in Christ. Amen.",
  penance="Give to the Society of Saint Vincent de Paul or another charity for the poor, or offer an hour of your time to someone in need.",
  meditation="Vincent told his sisters that to leave prayer to serve the poor is to leave God for God. Whose need might be God interrupting you today?",
  query="Saint Vincent de Paul painting Simon François"),
 "09-28": dict(name="Saint Wenceslaus", dates="c. 907–935 · Bohemia", match=["wenceslaus"], efMatch="Wenceslaus",
  history="Duke of Bohemia, raised in the faith by his grandmother Saint Ludmila. He ruled justly, built churches and cared for the poor, but his Christian rule and his peace with the Germans made him enemies. He was murdered by his brother Boleslav on his way to Mass. He is the patron saint of the Czech people.",
  life="Wenceslaus was the son of Duke Vratislav. After his father’s death his grandmother Ludmila brought him up a Christian, and in 921 she was murdered by nobles loyal to his mother, Drahomíra, who resented her influence. When he came of age Wenceslaus took up the government himself. He favoured the Church, welcomed priests from Bavaria, founded the rotunda of Saint Vitus in Prague and, to spare his land war, accepted the overlordship of the German king Henry the Fowler. Early lives tell of his charity, of his gathering wood for the poor by night and of his care for the bread and wine of the Mass. In 935, or perhaps 929, he went to Stará Boleslav at his brother’s invitation for the feast of Saints Cosmas and Damian. Next morning, on his way to Mass, he was struck down at the church door. The carol Good King Wenceslas is a Victorian invention, but true to his reputation.",
  prayerTo="Saint Wenceslaus, you ruled as a servant of Christ and met death on your way to Mass. Pray for all who hold power, that they may seek peace and justice, for families divided by envy and ambition, and for me, that I may love the Mass and be generous to the poor in their need. Amen.",
  penance="Give warm clothing, food or help with heating costs to someone in need today, and pray for reconciliation in a divided family.",
  meditation="Wenceslaus was killed by his own brother at the door of a church. Is there a brother or sister you need to forgive or be reconciled with?",
  query="Saint Wenceslaus Master Theodoric"),
 "09-29": dict(name="Saints Michael, Gabriel and Raphael", dates="Archangels · Michaelmas", match=["michael"], efMatch="Michael",
  history="The three angels named in Scripture. Michael, whose name asks who is like God, leads the heavenly host against Satan. Gabriel, the strength of God, brought Mary the news of the Incarnation. Raphael, God heals, guided Tobias and cured his father’s blindness. In England the day has long been kept as Michaelmas, a quarter day and the start of the autumn term.",
  life="The Church teaches that the angels are real, spiritual creatures who serve God and watch over us. Scripture names three. Michael appears in Daniel as the great prince who stands guard over God’s people, in the Letter of Jude disputing with the devil, and in the Apocalypse casting the dragon out of heaven. Gabriel is sent to Daniel to explain his visions, to Zechariah in the Temple to announce the birth of John the Baptist, and to Nazareth to greet Mary. Raphael, in the Book of Tobit, travels in disguise with the young Tobias, protects him and heals his father, then reveals himself as one of the seven who stand before the Lord. The date recalls the dedication of a basilica of Saint Michael on the Via Salaria near Rome in the early centuries. Since 1970 the three archangels have shared one feast.",
  prayer="Saint Michael the Archangel, defend us in battle. Be our protection against the wickedness and snares of the devil. May God rebuke him, we humbly pray; and do thou, O Prince of the heavenly host, by the power of God, thrust into hell Satan and all the evil spirits who wander through the world for the ruin of souls. Amen.",
  prayerSource="Pope Leo XIII, 1886",
  prayerTo="Saint Michael, defend us in the battle against evil. Saint Gabriel, help us to hear the word of God and answer it as Mary did. Saint Raphael, guide us on our journeys and bring healing to the sick. Holy archangels, and my own guardian angel, keep me close to God this day and all the days of my life. Amen.",
  penance="Pray the Saint Michael prayer today, and do a kindness for someone who is travelling or sick.",
  meditation="Michael’s name is a question: who is like God? Is there anything in your life that you have set up as though it were God?",
  query="Saint Michael Archangel painting Raphael"),
 "09-30": dict(name="Saint Jerome", dates="c. 347–420 · Dalmatia, Rome and Bethlehem", match=["jerome"], efMatch="Jerome",
  history="The greatest biblical scholar of the early Church. A brilliant, prickly priest from Dalmatia, he learned Hebrew in the Syrian desert, served Pope Damasus in Rome, and spent his last thirty-four years in Bethlehem translating the Scriptures into Latin. His Vulgate was the Bible of the Western Church for more than a thousand years.",
  life="Jerome was born at Stridon, on the borders of Dalmatia, and studied in Rome, where he was baptised. A great lover of the pagan classics, he later told of a dream in which he was judged a Ciceronian rather than a Christian. He tried the hermit’s life in the desert of Chalcis and there learned Hebrew from a Jewish convert. Ordained priest at Antioch, he came to Rome in 382 and became secretary to Pope Damasus, who asked him to revise the Latin Gospels. There he guided a circle of devout Roman women, among them Paula and her daughter Eustochium. After Damasus died he left for the Holy Land, and from 386 lived at Bethlehem, where Paula built monasteries for men and women. He translated most of the Old Testament from the Hebrew, wrote commentaries and letters, and quarrelled fiercely with his critics. He died at Bethlehem in 420.",
  prayerTo="Saint Jerome, you gave your life to the word of God and fought your own fierce temper all your days. Pray for translators, scholars and all who teach the Scriptures, and for me, that I may read the Bible daily, love it as you did, and bring my faults humbly to the Lord. Amen.",
  penance="Read a chapter of the Bible slowly today, perhaps a Gospel or a psalm, and hold back the sharp word when you are tempted to speak it.",
  meditation="Ignorance of the Scriptures is ignorance of Christ. How well do you know the Gospels, and how could you come to know them better?",
  query="Saint Jerome in his study painting Antonello da Messina"),
}
